{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQpSpHvc9VonzREwq8V5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your answer settles questions 1 and 2. You didn't say how far apart the installments are spaced, so I've held that until question 3 is answered. Choosing to let customers pick also raises a new question about which invoices qualify.\n\n**Settled so far**\n- The code-based points from last round still stand.\n- Customers choose the plan themselves.\n- They pick 2, 3, or 4 equal installments.\n\n**Choices I'll make myself:** the same as last round. Leftover cents go on the last installment, payments fill the earliest unpaid installment first, there's one reminder per installment, and the invoice becomes `paid` once the full total is paid.\n\n**Left out:** interest or fees for installments, changing a plan after it starts, and UI.\n\n**Questions**\n\n1. **Can the plan run past the invoice's original due date?**\n   - (a) No. All installments fall on or before the original due date. This only splits the payment, but an invoice due in a week leaves very little room for 4 installments.\n   - (b) Yes. The first installment is due on the original due date and the rest follow after it. This extends credit and changes when you actually get paid.\n   - *I recommend (b)* now that customers choose. Under (a) the option is close to meaningless for invoices that are due soon.\n\n2. **What happens when a customer misses an installment?**\n   - (a) The whole invoice becomes `overdue` as soon as any installment passes its date unpaid.\n   - (b) Only that installment is overdue. The invoice keeps its current status unless the final date is missed.\n   - *I recommend (a).* It keeps the current single-status model and the existing overdue flow working unchanged.\n\n3. **When can a customer choose installments?**\n   - (a) Only while the invoice is `sent`, nothing has been paid yet, and the due date hasn't passed.\n   - (b) At any point before the invoice is paid, including after it becomes overdue. This lets customers avoid a late fee they've already incurred.\n   - *I recommend (a).* It's simpler, and it keeps installments from becoming a way around the overdue rules.\n\n**Waiting on your answers**\n- How far apart the installments are (monthly, every 2 weeks, or spread evenly up to the due date) waits on question 1.\n- How the late fee applies to an installment invoice (2% of the full total once, or 2% of each missed installment) waits on question 2."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1900,"cache_read_input_tokens":33826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1900},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e917516-68bf-49d3-b443-7920d3c2e1f8","uuid":"01e8a6ba-9854-4d10-a072-80031751d5e4","timestamp":"2026-11-26T03:20:23.470Z","request_id":"req_011ChQpSp4Xzg8kPay7Z4h4D"}
